        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acpx): cap Pi session reads after file growth",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users reading local or paired-node Pi session transcripts through the ACPX plugin could still read an oversized session file into memory when the JSONL file grew after the 32 MiB size preflight.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe ACPX Pi session store now validates and reads the already-opened session file through one bounded handle, retaining at most the 32 MiB transcript budget plus one overflow byte before rejecting. This keeps the fix inside the Pi session catalog owner boundary without changing ACP runtime behavior, plugin config, or session catalog contracts.\n\n## User Impact\n\nPi session transcript reads now enforce the existing read safety limit against the bytes actually read, so rapidly growing session files fail with the intended size error instead of bypassing the cap.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Claim proved: ACPX Pi transcript reads reject a real temporary Pi JSONL session file that grows past the existing 32 MiB read safety limit before the transcript read completes.\n- Current-head proof at `a8c7ebafaeb8b05efb46d305f9d6e9b2df1ddeab`: `node --import tsx --input-type=module -` ran an unmocked runtime script through `readLocalPiTranscriptPage`; the script seeded a real session file at 33,550,336 bytes, appended 8,192 bytes with `fs.appendFile`, observed the file at 33,558,528 bytes against the 33,554,432-byte limit, and received `Pi session exceeds the 32 MiB read safety limit`.\n- Focused regression: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/acpx/src/pi-session-catalog.test.ts -t \"keeps the Pi session read safety limit when the file grows after preflight\"` passed with 1 test passed and 18 skipped.\n- Targeted format: `node_modules\\\\.bin\\\\oxfmt.cmd --check extensions\\\\acpx\\\\src\\\\pi-session-catalog.test.ts extensions\\\\acpx\\\\src\\\\pi-session-store.ts` passed.\n- PR diff hygiene: `git diff --check` passed.\n",
